{
 "note": "**The 1891 round, Cavendish Crescent South and Lenton Road - schedules 63 to 67 and 170 to 177.** RG12/2682, transcribed by A. Hagues. **Read to close the 1891 holes**, which [[every-census-gap-and-where-to-start]] now puts ahead of everything else.\n\n**Nine holes closed outright**, each a house the record held in 1881 and again in 1901 or 1911 but not in between:\n\n| schedule | house | who |\n|---|---|---|\n| 170 | **25 Lenton Road** | Hannah Maria Hopkins, her brother and two sisters - the household [[every-census-gap-and-where-to-start]] named as one of the six with the same family on both sides |\n| 171a | **29 Lenton Road** | Frederick Carver, lace manufacturer |\n| 172 | **31 Lenton Road** | Henry Clarke, lace manufacturer, at 48 in 1881 and 58 here |\n| 174 | **Cliff House** | George Parr, solicitor, with **eight children, two nieces and five servants** - seventeen in the house |\n| 175 | **Rock House, 37 Lenton Road** | Charles Shaw, 75, living on own means, who was 65 here in 1881 |\n| 64 | **21 Cavendish Crescent South** | Mary Ann Allen, 80, and her daughter Sarah, 53 - the second of the six named as having the same family either side. Richard Allen the bookseller has died |\n| 65 | **19 Cavendish Crescent South** | Alfred Page, haberdasher |\n| 66 | **17 Cavendish Crescent South** | Kate Green, 27, keeping house for nobody but two servants |\n| 67 | **15 Cavendish Crescent South** | Maria Bell, widow, 38, with five children |\n\n**Schedule 63 is 23 Cavendish Crescent South** - Holroyd House, where James Cloak the grocer was in 1881 at 67 and is here at 76, with six grown children still at home. That house already held 1891, so this only fills it out.\n\n**The numbers at the top of the page are hard to read** - 25, 27 and 29 could each be taken for 75, 77 and 79. They are settled by the households themselves: the Hopkins are at 25 in 1881 and 1901, Samuel Waite Johnson is at Lenton House, 27, in every other round, and the run is continuous.\n\n**Three households on these pages are deliberately left out**, because the record already holds them for 1891: Samuel Waite Johnson at **Lenton House (27 Lenton Road)**, James Bennett Gayton at **1 Holles Crescent**, and William Barrett at **1 Park Drive**.\n\n**One cannot be filed.** Schedule 180 gives **5 Park Drive** and the record has no number 5 - Park Drive runs 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9. The household is Margaret King Allen, widow, whom the 1881 round puts at **3** Park Drive with the same daughter ten years younger. Left for now rather than moved on a guess.\n\n**Joseph Newbold the coachman** carries a street and no number, so he is unfiled. He was a coachman in The Park in 1871 too, in John Adams's household.",
 "source": "1891 census enumerator's book RG12/2682, Lenton Road and Holles Crescent, transcribed by A. Hagues.",
